findings.body:
**Claimed critical; verifier found:** verifier off

This removes the allowlist and always sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to the request `origin` (or `*`) together with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`.

**Failure mode:** a malicious page at `https://evil.example` sends a credentialed XHR/fetch to this API. The response includes `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://evil.example` and `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, so the browser exposes the response body (and accepts cookie-authenticated side effects the attacker can observe). Any logged-in user visiting the attacker page is compromised.

Native mobile clients do not rely on CORS; webviews that do should be added to an explicit allowlist (or use bearer tokens without cookie credentials), not mirrored unconditionally.

Also, `origin ?? "*"` with credentials is invalid per the CORS spec when `origin` is null — browsers reject that pair — but the reflected-origin branch is the exploitable path.

```suggestion
  const ALLOWED = new Set(["https://app.example.com"]);
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return { Vary: "Origin" };
```
